22:03:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That figure could be plausible depending on the study, year, and definitions used. Some research on U.S. law-enforcement personnel has found that a large majority fall into the “overweight or obese” BMI categories—sometimes around 70–80% combined. But that does not mean 80% are necessarily unable to do their jobs or visibly “out of shape.” A few important caveats: - **“Overweight” includes BMI 25–29.9**, not just obesity. It can include people with relatively modest extra weight, and BMI can misclassify muscular people. - **Studies may cover a particular agency, region, or demographic**, rather than every officer nationally. - **The profession has real risk factors**: rotating shifts, sleep disruption, stress, long periods sitting in vehicles, limited healthy food options, and injury. - The relevant operational question is often **functional fitness**—strength, endurance, mobility, and ability to perform job tasks—not appearance alone. So you’re not necessarily inventing the trend: some evidence does suggest elevated rates of excess weight among officers. It’s just better framed as an occupational health issue affecting many officers, rather than a reliable judgment about every individual cop.
